🌸 IRIS Dataset Insights
Baseline Accuracy (Supervised with 10% labeled data): Perfect classification.
Self-Training reached high accuracy (93.33%) and remained stable over iterations.
Co-Training showed good early performance but decreased slightly after adding noisy labels.
Label Propagation failed (33.33%), likely due to:
The algorithm’s assumption of smooth label transitions.
Difficulty handling well-separated but overlapping multi-class distributions.
✅ Best Semi-Supervised Method: Self-Training
❌ Least Effective: Label Propagation

🧬 PIMA Diabetes Dataset Insights
Baseline Accuracy (with 10% labeled data): 64.71%
Self-Training improved performance slightly to 66.67%.
Co-Training and Label Propagation reached 65.36%, showing minor improvement over the baseline.
Accuracy fluctuated across iterations, suggesting that:
The benefit of unlabeled data was limited.
Adding pseudo-labeled examples introduced some noise.
The dataset may have overlapping or noisy class boundaries.
✅ Best Semi-Supervised Method: Self-Training (by a small margin)
⚠️ Co-Training and Label Propagation performed similarly, but with no significant edge over baseline.

🔎 General Conclusions
Self-Training is the most reliable approach across both datasets.
Co-Training is sensitive to feature splits and pseudo-label noise.
Label Propagation requires well-structured feature space and works better for binary classification (like PIMA).
Semi-supervised learning helps most when:
The labeled dataset is small,
The unlabeled data is clean and well-separated,
And the model can avoid error reinforcement when labeling new samples.
